INT. CORPORATE OFFICE - 31ST FLOOR - MORNING

An open-plan floor of the sort that wins architecture awards and loses employees. Glass walls. Beanbags nobody sits in. A kombucha tap that dispenses nothing. In the corner office, behind a desk the size of a landing strip, sits HORTENSE BELLWEATHER, fifties, in a charcoal suit and a necklace of tiny silver clocks. She has the posture of a woman who has survived four mergers.

On the desk, a speakerphone the colour of an old tooth. Beside it, a cold cup of tea.

She stares at the phone. It blinks green.

HORTENSE
Archibald. Speak.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
(over speakerphone, jaunty)
Hortense! Morning! Lovely morning. Are you looking out the window?

HORTENSE
I'm looking at my screen.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Look out the window.

She turns her chair. Across the plaza, in the opposing tower, a man in a cream suit waves from the thirty-first floor. ARCHIBALD DRUMMOND, sixties, silver hair, big teeth. He holds his own phone to his ear and waves with the other hand.

HORTENSE
You moved your desk to face mine.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Corporate suggested "transparency."

HORTENSE
Corporate suggested "dialogue."

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Transparency is dialogue with glass.

Hortense sets her tea down. She does not smile. The clocks on her necklace jingle.

HORTENSE
What do you want?

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
A little chat. About the Henderson account.

HORTENSE
The Henderson account is mine.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Is it?

HORTENSE
I won it.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
You did. In 2016.

She taps a pen on the desk. Across the plaza, Archibald pulls a thick binder from a drawer and holds it up to the glass, like a magician showing an empty hat.

HORTENSE
What's that?

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
The reorganisation.

HORTENSE
I wasn't consulted.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
You were cc'd.

HORTENSE
On a Friday at five.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Best day for news.

She rises and crosses to the window, pressing a hand to the cold glass. Down below, the plaza fountain is dry, its basin full of cigarette butts and coins. A pigeon bathes in the dust.

HORTENSE
Under the plan, who reports to whom?

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
You report to me.

HORTENSE
Say that again.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
You. Report. To me.

Hortense nods slowly. She returns to her chair. She picks up the speakerphone and brings it close, until her mouth is inches from the grille.

HORTENSE
Archibald. How many years have you worked here?

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Nineteen.

HORTENSE
And how many have I?

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Thirty-one.

HORTENSE
Thirty-one. Do you know what I've learned?

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
That silence is golden?

HORTENSE
That people with binders are afraid of people without them.

Across the plaza, Archibald's smile flickers. He lowers the binder.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
Hortense, this isn't personal.

HORTENSE
It's the most personal thing in this building.

She opens a drawer. Withdraws a single sheet of paper and a gold fountain pen. She uncaps the pen with a small, decisive click. She writes. The nib scratches. Across the way, Archibald leans forward, squinting.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
What are you doing?

HORTENSE
Redistributing.

She holds the sheet to the window. Large block letters, readable even at forty yards: I RESIGN. EFFECTIVE IMMEDIATELY. TAKE THE KOMBUCHA.

Archibald's mouth opens. The binder slips from his hands. He bends to retrieve it and bumps his head on the desk.

ARCHIBALD (V.O.)
(muffled, wincing)
Hortense!

HORTENSE
Goodbye, Archibald.

She presses the button. The line clicks dead. She gathers her tea, her pen, and a single silver clock from the necklace, which she unhooks and places on the desk like a chess piece.

Then she walks out, heels ringing on the polished floor, past the empty beanbags and the silent tap, and into the elevator.

Across the plaza, Archibald presses his face to the glass. Behind him, the floor has gone very quiet.
